WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:06.530
Today light is doing something rather surprising: it can knock electrons out

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:06.640
of

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:06.980
a metal.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.360
This is the photoelectric effect.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.191
Think of an electron as needing an energy payment before it can leave the

00:00:14.191 --> 00:00:14.620
surface.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.980
Making the light brighter does not necessarily make that payment large enough.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.360
Hertz observed that ultraviolet light influenced electrical discharge.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.160
Later, Lenard studied the emitted electrons and how their energy responded to

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:28.540
the light.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.760
The important observations are these.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.100
Below a threshold frequency, ordinary photoelectric emission does not occur,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.700
however bright the light is in the usual single photon model.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.540
Above threshold, emission can begin promptly, without a measurable waiting

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.020
period for electrons to accumulate energy.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.260
Increasing frequency increases the maximum electron energy.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.820
Increasing intensity at a fixed suitable frequency mainly supplies more

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.280
photons, rather than making each photon more energetic.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:00.640
Pause here.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.920
Would a brighter red lamp necessarily release electrons from a surface that

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.120
responds to ultraviolet light?

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:06.980
No.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.980
Brightness and photon energy are different quantities.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.860
Einstein explained these observations by treating light's energy transfer as

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.500
discrete packets called photons.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.340
Each photon carries energy h times frequency.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.040
This particle like interaction does not erase light's wave behavior in

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.240
interference or diffraction.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.200
Different experiments reveal different aspects.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.960
For JEE, keep those three observations separate: threshold, prompt emission,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.760
and the distinct roles of frequency and intensity.

00:01:38.112 --> 00:01:40.712
Let's turn the energy payment into an equation.

00:01:41.492 --> 00:01:44.212
A photon transfers energy to an electron.

00:01:44.912 --> 00:01:48.592
The electron first needs the work function, phi, to escape the surface.

00:01:49.292 --> 00:01:51.692
Whatever remains can appear as kinetic energy.

00:01:52.452 --> 00:01:57.412
For the most energetic emitted electrons, K maximum equals h f minus phi.

00:01:58.452 --> 00:02:02.072
Other emitted electrons can have less energy because of their initial states

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:04.092
and interactions inside the material.

00:02:05.042 --> 00:02:09.272
The work function is a property of the particular surface, not a universal

00:02:09.272 --> 00:02:10.552
constant for all metals.

00:02:11.592 --> 00:02:15.692
When wavelength is given, photon energy is h c divided by wavelength.

00:02:15.692 --> 00:02:20.572
Here is a practical shortcut: h c is approximately twelve hundred forty

00:02:20.572 --> 00:02:22.372
electron volts times nanometers.

00:02:23.492 --> 00:02:27.512
Suppose phi is two electron volts and the incident wavelength is four hundred

00:02:27.512 --> 00:02:28.052
nanometers.

00:02:29.372 --> 00:02:33.232
Photon energy is twelve hundred forty divided by four hundred, or three point

00:02:33.232 --> 00:02:34.352
one electron volts.

00:02:35.532 --> 00:02:36.612
Subtract the escape energy.

00:02:37.352 --> 00:02:40.652
The maximum kinetic energy is one point one electron volts.

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:45.372
Your turn: could seven hundred nanometer light cause emission from this same

00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:45.572
surface?

00:02:46.252 --> 00:02:46.752
Take a moment.

00:02:47.352 --> 00:02:51.412
Its photon energy is about one point seven seven electron volts, below the

00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:52.072
required two.

00:02:52.352 --> 00:02:53.332
So there is no emission.

00:02:54.112 --> 00:02:55.832
Do not report a negative kinetic energy.

00:02:56.412 --> 00:02:59.312
At threshold the maximum kinetic energy reaches zero.

00:02:59.872 --> 00:03:04.139
The longest wavelength that can just satisfy the energy requirement is twelve

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:07.492
hundred forty divided by two, or six hundred twenty nanometers.

00:03:08.452 --> 00:03:10.632
Shorter wavelengths have higher photon energies.

00:03:11.372 --> 00:03:13.832
Longer wavelengths fail the threshold condition in this model.

00:03:14.232 --> 00:03:17.932
Now imagine two lamps of the same color shining on the same surface.

00:03:18.492 --> 00:03:18.992
One is brighter.

00:03:19.712 --> 00:03:23.112
Because their frequencies match, their photons carry the same individual

00:03:23.112 --> 00:03:23.472
energy.

00:03:24.172 --> 00:03:26.592
The brighter lamp supplies more photons each second.

00:03:27.292 --> 00:03:31.052
If emission is possible and the collection conditions are suitable, more

00:03:31.052 --> 00:03:34.612
electrons can be emitted and collected, giving a larger photocurrent.

00:03:35.312 --> 00:03:39.232
But the maximum energy of an emitted electron does not increase merely because

00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:40.832
more identical photons arrive.

00:03:40.832 --> 00:03:43.252
Here is a tiny numerical example.

00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:47.860
At a fixed frequency, doubling incident power doubles the incident photon

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.132
rate.

00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:52.652
If quantum efficiency and collection remain unchanged, the collected current

00:03:52.652 --> 00:03:53.592
doubles too.

00:03:54.272 --> 00:03:58.052
That proportionality needs those assumptions; it is not a promise for every

00:03:58.052 --> 00:04:00.092
real surface at every operating condition.

00:04:00.832 --> 00:04:02.392
Now change the color instead.

00:04:03.152 --> 00:04:05.092
Increasing frequency raises the energy per photon.

00:04:05.092 --> 00:04:08.532
With the same work function, that raises K maximum.

00:04:09.312 --> 00:04:13.572
However, if total power is fixed, increasing photon energy reduces the number

00:04:13.572 --> 00:04:14.992
of photons arriving per second.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:18.152
Real emission efficiency can also change with frequency.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:22.432
Therefore, never automatically say that photocurrent must increase with

00:04:22.432 --> 00:04:22.732
frequency.

00:04:23.512 --> 00:04:24.692
Separate energy from rate.

00:04:25.432 --> 00:04:29.233
Think for a second: same frequency, three times the intensity, above

00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:29.792
threshold.

00:04:30.432 --> 00:04:31.772
What happens to the stopping potential?

00:04:32.252 --> 00:04:36.538
It stays the same in the ideal model, although the saturation current can

00:04:36.538 --> 00:04:36.832
rise.

00:04:37.452 --> 00:04:41.592
Below threshold, more ordinary single photon illumination still cannot solve

00:04:41.592 --> 00:04:42.432
the energy deficit.

00:04:43.172 --> 00:04:47.152
This distinction is one of the most useful photoelectric traps to recognize in

00:04:47.152 --> 00:04:48.092
a JEE question.

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:52.768
How do we measure the maximum electron energy without following individual

00:04:52.768 --> 00:04:53.248
electrons?

00:04:53.888 --> 00:04:55.448
We use a collector and a voltage.

00:04:56.188 --> 00:04:58.968
A sufficiently positive collector attracts emitted electrons.

00:04:59.808 --> 00:05:03.468
As collection becomes complete, the current approaches a saturation value.

00:05:04.208 --> 00:05:07.816
A negative collector instead pushes electrons back toward the emitting

00:05:07.816 --> 00:05:08.228
surface.

00:05:09.188 --> 00:05:12.751
Increase this retarding voltage until even the fastest emitted electrons

00:05:12.751 --> 00:05:13.048
cannot

00:05:13.048 --> 00:05:14.258
reach the collector.

00:05:14.258 --> 00:05:18.488
That voltage magnitude is the stopping potential, V s.

00:05:18.488 --> 00:05:22.988
The electric energy barrier is electron charge magnitude times V s.

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:26.728
So K maximum equals e V s.

00:05:26.728 --> 00:05:30.888
Notice our convention: V s is positive as a magnitude, even though the

00:05:30.888 --> 00:05:33.348
collector voltage relative to the emitter is negative at stopping.

00:05:34.068 --> 00:05:37.968
In a current versus collector voltage graph, the stopping intercept therefore

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:39.528
lies on the negative voltage side.

00:05:40.168 --> 00:05:43.688
Return to the four hundred nanometer example with a two electron volt work

00:05:43.688 --> 00:05:43.948
function.

00:05:44.748 --> 00:05:47.528
The maximum kinetic energy was one point one electron volts.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:50.708
The stopping magnitude is one point one volts.

00:05:51.608 --> 00:05:55.028
No conversion to joules is required if we use the electron volt definition

00:05:55.028 --> 00:05:55.608
consistently.

00:05:56.408 --> 00:06:00.268
If we do use joules, divide the energy by the elementary charge to obtain

00:06:00.268 --> 00:06:00.628
volts.

00:06:01.468 --> 00:06:04.228
Now picture two intensity curves at the same frequency.

00:06:04.948 --> 00:06:08.848
The brighter light has the higher saturation current, but both curves meet the

00:06:08.848 --> 00:06:10.568
voltage axis at the same stopping intercept.

00:06:11.528 --> 00:06:15.208
At a higher frequency, that intercept shifts farther into negative collector

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:15.608
voltage.

00:06:16.368 --> 00:06:20.248
Energy changes move the stopping position; photon rate changes mainly change

00:06:20.248 --> 00:06:20.628
the height.

00:06:20.808 --> 00:06:24.528
Let's finish with the graph that turns the whole chapter into one line.

00:06:25.628 --> 00:06:29.188
Plot maximum kinetic energy vertically and frequency horizontally.

00:06:30.128 --> 00:06:34.008
Above threshold, Einstein's equation gives a straight line with slope h.

00:06:34.908 --> 00:06:38.328
Its frequency axis intercept is phi divided by h.

00:06:39.068 --> 00:06:43.008
Extending the algebraic line backward gives a negative vertical intercept, but

00:06:43.008 --> 00:06:45.928
that extension is not a physical branch of emitted electrons.

00:06:46.568 --> 00:06:50.608
Below threshold there are no emitted electrons to assign that negative kinetic

00:06:50.608 --> 00:06:51.348
energy to.

00:06:52.068 --> 00:06:55.588
For the stopping potential graph, the slope is h divided by e.

00:06:56.388 --> 00:06:58.568
Here is an original JEE style example.

00:06:59.588 --> 00:07:03.214
Use h approximately four point one four times ten to the minus fifteen

00:07:03.214 --> 00:07:03.628
electron

00:07:03.628 --> 00:07:04.208
volt seconds.

00:07:05.008 --> 00:07:07.228
A surface has work function two electron volts.

00:07:08.088 --> 00:07:12.348
At frequency six times ten to the fourteen hertz, photon energy is two point

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:13.548
four eight four electron volts.

00:07:14.488 --> 00:07:15.128
Subtract two.

00:07:15.128 --> 00:07:19.508
K maximum is zero point four eight four electron volts, and the stopping

00:07:19.508 --> 00:07:21.888
magnitude is zero point four eight four volts.

00:07:22.868 --> 00:07:26.608
At eight times ten to the fourteen hertz, photon energy is three point three

00:07:26.608 --> 00:07:27.648
one two electron volts.

00:07:28.708 --> 00:07:31.388
The stopping magnitude becomes one point three one two volts.

00:07:32.368 --> 00:07:35.528
Before calculating anything new, predict the direction of the change.

00:07:36.628 --> 00:07:40.088
Higher frequency means a larger surplus after paying the same escape cost.

00:07:41.228 --> 00:07:41.508
Good.

00:07:41.508 --> 00:07:45.708
When two surfaces have different work functions, their energy frequency lines

00:07:45.708 --> 00:07:48.308
have the same slope but different thresholds.

00:07:49.408 --> 00:07:53.648
Always identify which variable changed, check emission first, and only then

00:07:53.648 --> 00:07:54.408
apply the equation.
